menu "CAN (canbus)"

    config CANBUS_RX_QUEUE_LEN
        int "Frames the receive queue holds"
        default 256
        range 16 1024
        help
            Between the controller's interrupt and whoever reads the frames (the `can` command,
            or the Cyphal node). 24 bytes each, in internal RAM, from the first start on.

            A flash write holds the reader off while the interrupt goes on filling the queue:
            an erase runs for up to CONFIG_SPI_FLASH_ERASE_YIELD_DURATION_MS (20 ms) before it
            yields. Measured 2026-11-20 with a bus saturated at 1 Mbit/s by 8-byte frames
            (about 8,100 a second) during a 1 MB LittleFS write (`fs bench 1024`): the queue
            held 155 and 164 frames at most in two runs, and a 128-frame queue dropped 997 frames
            over the write (holo-player-fw's ESP32-S3). On the dome panel's ESP32-P4 (2026-11-21),
            a bus saturated by the adapter at ~8,800 frames a second across 100 console commands,
            each seven 4 KB erases, held 20 at most and dropped none. 256 covers both with room.
            A bus saturated with shorter frames (up to about 21,000 a second with no data) would
            need more; a Cyphal bus is normally far lighter.

    config CANBUS_TX_QUEUE_LEN
        int "Frames waiting to be sent"
        default 16
        range 2 255
        help
            Frames given to canbus_send() and not yet on the bus. When they are all waiting
            (nobody acknowledging, or bus-off), canbus_send() waits for room or times out. The `can`
            command uses them all; the Cyphal node one (canbus_config_t.tx_slots), keeping its
            own queue in priority order in front.

    config CANBUS_CMD_TASK_STACK
        int "`can` reader task: stack (bytes)"
        default 3072
        range 2048 16384
        help
            The task `can start` makes to read the receive queue, echo and print. Internal RAM,
            from `can start` to `can stop` only. 3072 is what it has run on since it was written.
            Measured on the ESP32-P4 (2026-11-22, `tasks` after 17,000 frames echoed with `can
            dump` printing 3,000 of them): 2268 bytes left at the low-water mark, so ~800 used.

    config CANBUS_CMD_TASK_PRIO
        int "`can` reader task: priority"
        default 6
        range 1 24
        help
            Above the console's, so an echo is not held behind a command the console is
            printing. The default is the first application's (holo-player-fw), where it sits
            over that board's video player at 5.

    config CANBUS_CMD_TASK_CORE
        int "`can` reader task: core"
        default 0
        range 0 1
        help
            Core 0 is the console's on both targets; the first application (holo-player-fw)
            keeps core 1 for its video player.

endmenu
